PowerPC interpreter sources from the local SheepShaver reference checkout:
D:/Macintosh/source/macemu/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu (read 2026-10-18).
Kheperix by Gwenole Beauchesne, GPL-2.0-or-later; see COPYING and file headers.

Executor uses the portable interpreter, without upstream JIT or decode caching.
Local changes: portable member-function dispatch avoids executable host thunks;
CPU memory accesses use Executor callbacks; illegal instructions raise a caught
execution error. Invalid update-register forms also raise a caught error. Executor-specific configuration/adapter lives outside this
third-party directory. Generated execution instantiations belong in obj/.

Executor builds the invariant opcode decoder once, then copies it into each
CPU instance before installing that instance's host-service instruction.
Nested CPUs retain independent mutable decoder tables. This reduces repeated
setup work; it is not a JIT or an instruction-content cache.

An optional hook before instruction dispatch calls Executor's original bounded
IA-32/x86-64 integer-block emitter in src/ppc-jit.cpp. That backend is independent of
the upstream generator, which is not imported. Windows Executor enables these
blocks by default; the standalone API requires an explicit opt-in. Interpretation
handles instructions outside the supported subset.

Executor also routes multiply-add/subtract value computation through original
exact binary arithmetic in src/ppc-fused.cpp, using explicit FPSCR rounding.
The adapter applies negative forms after rounding and classifies raw results.
This avoids intermediate rounding in the generic mathlib and the Windows CRT.
The upstream host-fenv exception machinery stays disabled. Executor adapters
provide arithmetic status; MSR-controlled exception delivery remains unfinished.

Ordinary arithmetic and FRSP values route through src/ppc-arithmetic.cpp and
the separately vendored Berkeley SoftFloat 3e dependency. FSQRT/FSQRTS decoding
is enabled for Executor, with reserved operand fields rejected. The adapter
preserves PowerPC NaN preference and maps FPSCR rounding directions. Ordinary
and fused adapters update FPSCR, including scaled range results and rounding
metadata; MSR-controlled exception delivery remains unfinished.

FCTIW/FCTIWZ use the software adapter for values and conversion status rather
than host lrint/casts. Their local path updates FR/FI, sticky causes and FX,
derives VX/FEX, preserves invalid-enabled destinations and records CR1 after
the status update. Reserved FRA fields are rejected before operand access.
This does not turn on the upstream host-fenv exception implementation or add
MSR-controlled floating-point trap delivery.

FCMPU/FCMPO similarly use raw-bit comparisons through the adapter, preserving
NaN payloads and reporting comparison exceptions without host fenv dependence.
They update FPCC and the selected CR field and reject reserved encodings.

Executor uses generic C math on hosts whose optional arch-specific headers are
not part of this imported subset. Native round-mode changes route through its
x87/SSE synchronization helper; x86-64 environments explicitly preserve MXCSR
because the tested MinGW C environment functions omitted part of that state.

Non-arithmetic FP moves/sign/select instructions use integer register bits in
Executor, and LFS expands the IEEE representation without host FP arithmetic.
This prevents unintended SNaN quieting and leaves FPSCR unchanged. Entry/return
copies of the public register file also preserve the original representation.

Ordinary arithmetic and FRSP additionally call the original adapter's raw-bit
exception classifier for VXSNAN, VXISI, VXIDI, VXZDZ, VXIMZ, VXSQRT and ZX.
It updates sticky summaries, clears FR/FI for these causes and inhibits writes
under VE/ZE; record forms still update CR1. Other ordinary arithmetic status,
scaled enabled-exception results and MSR trap delivery remain unfinished.

Fused forms use the same raw-bit status adapter for VXSNAN/VXIMZ/VXISI, including
combined signaling-NaN/addend and invalid-product cases (PEM Appendix D.2).
The adapter runs before NaN quieting, inhibits enabled-invalid writes and allows
valid fused results to update FPRF even when VE is set. Overflow/underflow,
inexact, rounding status and trap delivery remain separate unfinished work.

Ordinary arithmetic/FRSP now returns commit/inhibition status with FPSCR and
raw result classification from the adapter. OX/UX/XX, FR/FI and scaled OE/UE
results use target-precision extended-exponent operations when needed. The CPU
keeps those results in double-width FPR storage, including single-precision
scaled results beyond binary32 range. Raw operands are classified before any
host FP read, preserving signaling NaNs on x87 hosts. Fused rounding flags and
MSR exception delivery remain unfinished.

Fused arithmetic now uses the raw-bit value/status adapter too. Its exact integer
accumulator reports rounding and range metadata, including scaled OE/UE results.
The legacy host-double CPU helper was removed; the bits-only oracle wrapper still
provides its documented host-fenv interface. The CPU path classifies original
NaNs, preserves full payloads and does not read host floating flags. MSR trap
delivery remains unfinished.

FPSCR control writes now call the software summary adapter even with upstream
host-fenv exceptions disabled. MTFSFI correctly positions its immediate and can
write FX. MTFSB1 preserves old causes when deciding whether FX transitions, and
MCRFS recomputes summaries after copying/clearing causes. Reserved opcode fields
are rejected and reserved FPSCR bit 20 is not writable. Original instruction
tests cover masks, fields, all bits, record forms and source NaN preservation.

FRSQRTE also routes through the software value/status adapter. Its binary64
square root/reciprocal removes the upstream float narrowing and satisfies the
UISA estimate tolerance across the full exponent range. It handles raw NaNs,
invalid square roots, zero divides and enabled-result inhibition before host FP
reads. Undefined FR/FI bits are cleared, and no XX/OX/UX is raised by the estimate.

FRES uses the estimate adapter too, evaluating reciprocals with an extended
exponent range before binary32 conversion. It supplies finite scaled OE/UE
results, OX/UX and zero-divide/NaN status without host floating arithmetic. XX
is not raised by estimates, and undefined FR/FI are cleared consistently.

Integer comparisons reject the 750's unsupported 64-bit L forms and reserved
instruction bits before changing CR. Valid comparisons retain the upstream
signed/unsigned operand and CR-field implementation. Original fixtures also
check invalid rewrites of already compiled native blocks.

Rotate helpers mask both complementary shift counts, including zero rotations,
to avoid C++ shifts by 32. A whole-core ASan/UBSan build reproduces the upstream
fault and exercises the corrected instruction paths against original bit-model
fixtures; it does not change the guest rotate instruction results.

MULLI uses unsigned modulo arithmetic for its low-word product, avoiding the
upstream signed 32-bit C++ overflow. MULHW/MULHWU reject their reserved OE bit
before register or condition-code changes. Original widened-integer fixtures
and QEMU 750 vectors cover immediate and signed/unsigned high-word products;
the entire core also runs under ASan/UBSan.

Unary CNTLZW/EXTSB/EXTSH and NEG reject their unused RB field before reading
operands. MFCR rejects its reserved operand/Rc bits. Original fixtures check
2,357 invalid forms and valid unary values/record flags without changing the
existing arithmetic implementation.

ADDME/ADDZE/SUBFME/SUBFZE reject the unused RB field before operand reads,
including overflow-enabled forms. The native carry-chain fixture checks all
31 nonzero RB fields after they replace a warm native block; the interpreter
previously accepted these reserved encodings. Valid addition semantics remain
upstream, with independent widened-integer carry/record checks.

CR logical operations reject Rc=1, and MCRF rejects its unused operand/Rc
fields before changing CR. CR destination masks use an unsigned bit. Original
hardware probes preserve the classic ABI's nonvolatile CR fields; host fixtures
also check rejection after reserved words replace warmed native blocks.

MTCRF rejects unused operand/Rc bits, including the later one-field selector
unsupported by the 750. MCRXR rejects unused operand/Rc bits before copying or
clearing XER flags. Dedicated original hardware and host fixtures cover field
masks, flag clearing, register aliases and cold/hot invalid instruction words.

MFSPR/MTSPR reject reserved Rc before SPR access or register changes. Original
user-SPR hardware probes constrain XER data to defined bits, since the 603's
reserved-bit retention is not an architecture oracle. Native code preserves
the existing interpreted filtering policy for nonzero reserved XER data.

Raw FPR move/sign integration rejects reserved FRA fields in FMR/FNEG/FABS/
FNABS before reading or changing register state (EXECUTOR_PPC only). The native
adapter compiles these forms as integer copies/sign masks and records live
FPSCR to CR1 for Rc; it does not use host floating operations.

DCBZ rejects the unused register field and Rc before computing the effective
address or clearing memory (EXECUTOR_PPC only). Valid flat-RAM block clearing
retains the upstream behavior; this does not implement a physical data cache.

The Executor JIT can resolve the current CPU's adapted instruction descriptor
through executor_instruction. Native semantic tails use those ahead-compiled
kernels after committing prefix state and counters. No decoder/CPU address is
persisted, and no generated call frame survives callbacks or fault unwinding.
This does not add privileged state, exception vectors or MMU translation to the
upstream application-level interpreter.

Executor announces the original CPU provenance once per process instead of on
every short-call CPU construction. The banner text and all source legal notices
are retained; independent and nested CPU state remains unchanged.

The Executor-owned cpu/vm.hpp adapter now lives under synppc/platform. Scalar
accesses use the legacy span adapter or the optional 750 physical bus. The
Executor-only instruction loop hook enters architectural exceptions at an
instruction boundary; execute_illegal throws a typed program fault in that
mode. executor_registers exposes existing state to the maintained supervisor
adapter without changing the register layout. Upstream legal notices remain.

Executor's MFSPR selectors 268/269 read the same time-base clock as MFTB, rather
than the generic unknown-SPR zero fallback. The architectural adapter uses its
caller-driven TB state; the legacy adapter keeps its existing host clock.

Executor-only memory-form guards use the adapter invalid-instruction hook so
architectural mode enters a guest program exception. The flat application
adapter retains the existing diagnostic strings for the same rejected forms.
